1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,346
Presenter: The children's book festival is coming up again soon

2
00:00:03,346 --> 00:00:07,908
and here to tell us all about it is the festival's organiser, Jenny Morgan.

3
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:10,950
So tell us what we can expect this year, Jenny.

4
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:17,904
Jenny: Well, as usual we've got five days of action-packed exciting events for children,

5
00:00:17,904 --> 00:00:22,540
with writers coming from all over the country getting involved.

6
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:28,279
Just to give you an idea of what's on offer in the workshops First of all,

7
00:00:28,279 --> 00:00:30,980
there's a very special event called Superheroes.

8
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:38,370
This is a chance for deaf children to share their reading experiences with author Madeleine Gordon,

9
00:00:38,370 --> 00:00:40,680
who is herself hearing impaired.

10
00:00:40,680 --> 00:00:47,380
'Just do it' is a practical workshop led by the well-known illustrator Mark Keane.

11
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:52,285
He'll take participants on a magical journey to faraway lands

12
00:00:52,285 --> 00:00:57,190
with an opportunity for aspiring actors to do some role play.

13
00:00:57,190 --> 00:00:59,260
'Count on me' is an inspiring

14
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:04,940
and entertaining look at the issues of friendship for 13-14-year-olds.

15
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:09,649
It looks at some of the friendships described in popular books

16
00:01:09,649 --> 00:01:13,930
and asks participants to compare these with their own experiences.

17
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:19,930
'Speak up' is part of a series of workshops on the subject of mental health.

18
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:24,570
This is a creative writing workshop encouraging children to describe situations

19
00:01:24,570 --> 00:01:28,850
where young people experience loneliness.

20
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:32,890
A recent survey revealed that children can be lonely even

21
00:01:32,890 --> 00:01:36,160
when they're at home with their families.

22
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,770
'Jump for joy', as many of you will know, is the heart-warming,

23
00:01:40,770 --> 00:01:47,300
best-selling story by Nina Karan about a young girl's trip to visit her relatives in India.

24
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:51,743
It recently received the gold medal at the Waterford Awards.

25
00:01:51,743 --> 00:01:57,520
Nina will get children to celebrate the word 'joy' by writing a poem.

26
00:01:57,520 --> 00:02:04,280
'Sticks and stones' is the beautifully illustrated picture book for young readers about a community

27
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:10,590
who organise an African-Caribbean festival to help local children learn about their Jamaican roots.

28
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:12,550
This will be a musical event

29
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:17,113
where children will have the chance to play steel drums.

30
00:02:17,113 --> 00:02:22,820
This is bound to be very popular, so please book as soon as possible.

31
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:24,977
Presenter: That all sounds really interesting.

32
00:02:24,977 --> 00:02:30,730
I'm just wondering if you have a favourite book you could recommend for our readers?

33
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:36,600
Jenny: It's hard to choose, but Alive and Kicking is definitely worth mentioning.

34
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:41,990
You won't have heard of the writer as it's her first book - which is really impressive.

35
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:48,250
It's basically the teenage diary of a boy from Somalia who comes to live in the UK.

36
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,076
It deals with the serious issue of immigration

37
00:02:51,076 --> 00:02:54,130
and all the challenges the boy has to face at school

38
00:02:54,130 --> 00:02:57,080
and with the language barrier, etc.

39
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,990
Usually, books like this are quite sad

40
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:04,440
but this one actually made me cry with laughter.

41
00:03:04,440 --> 00:03:06,434
On each page, there are simple

42
00:03:06,434 --> 00:03:11,420
but hilarious black and white stick drawings of the boy with his friends and teachers.

43
00:03:11,420 --> 00:03:13,895
At the end of each diary entry,

44
00:03:13,895 --> 00:03:16,410
there are new English words the boy learns each day

45
00:03:16,810 --> 00:03:20,260
which may help develop some children's vocabulary.

46
00:03:20,260 --> 00:03:23,022
Presenter: I think my kids would enjoy that.

47
00:03:23,022 --> 00:03:28,200
What about any advice for parents on how to encourage their children to read more?

48
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:31,940
Jenny: Well, this is something I get asked about a lot.

49
00:03:31,940 --> 00:03:37,290
There are so many distractions for kids these days that it can be hard to find time for reading.

50
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:40,890
One thing I'd say is to make time to sit down with your child

51
00:03:40,890 --> 00:03:42,840
and share books with them.

52
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:48,785
A lot of parents give up reading aloud to their children as soon as they learn to read independently,

53
00:03:48,785 --> 00:03:50,350
but this is a mistake.

54
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:55,980
It's good to read more advanced books to them as it helps to develop their vocabulary.

55
00:03:55,980 --> 00:04:00,469
If you don't have time for this, then let them listen to audio books.

56
00:04:00,469 --> 00:04:04,360
Often, they'll want to read books they've listened to for themselves.

57
00:04:04,360 --> 00:04:10,512
I think it's a good idea to make a mental note of the type of books your child is reading -

58
00:04:10,512 --> 00:04:15,200
often they just read the same genre all the time, which can get a bit boring.

59
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,430
You can introduce new authors and genres to them

60
00:04:18,430 --> 00:04:21,270
Librarians should be able to help you with this.

61
00:04:21,270 --> 00:04:23,640
Presenter: Well Jenny, I think that's really useful....
